## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical lock design for multi-socket systems, balancing scalability and correctness under contention.

## Key Concepts

- MCS locks and queue-based synchronization
- NUMA-aware data locality
- Cache-line padding and false sharing avoidance
- Timeout/backoff to prevent livelock

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo C-like sketch for acquire/release
typedef struct Node { volatile int locked; struct Node *next; } Node;
typedef struct Lock { Node *tail; } MCSLock;
void lock_acquire(MCSLock *L, Node *me) {
  me->next = NULL; me->locked = 1;
  Node *pred = atomic_swap(&L->tail, me);
  if (pred) { pred->next = me; while (me->locked) pause(); }
}
void lock_release(MCSLock *L, Node *me) {
  if (!me->next) { if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&L->tail, me, NULL)) return; while (!me->next) pause(); }
  me->next->locked = 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend for reentrancy or per-thread timeouts?
- How does this scale when threads migrate between NUMA nodes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to reason about real-world paging under SLA constraints in multi-tenant containerized environments.

## Key Concepts

- Deadline-aware paging and QoS management
- Per-page lifecycle with CAS-based fault claiming
- Multi-tenant memory quotas and NUMA placement
- Tail-latency testing and eviction strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: handle_fault(page)
function handleFault(page) {
  if (!compareAndSwap(page.inflight, 0, 1)) return; // Another thread is handling it

  if (page.deadline < SHORT_THRESHOLD) {
    scheduleAsyncSSDRead(page, page.deadline);
  } else {
    if (cache.hasEvictablePage(page.serviceId)) {
      reuseEvictedPage(page);
    } else {
      zeroFill(page);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer mechanisms, wakeup latency, and concrete data structures suitable for embedded or real-time operating systems.

## Key Concepts
- Timers with absolute expiry and optional periodicity
- Heap-based scheduling for efficient expiry checks
- Interrupt-safe wakeups and drift prevention
- Latency testing with mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
function timer_add(heap, expiry, period, task) {
  push(heap, { expiry, period, task });
  siftUp(heap, heap.length - 1);
}

function timer_interrupt(now_ms) {
  while (heap.length > 0 && heap[0].expiry <= now_ms) {
    const timer = popMin(heap);
    timer.task();
    if (timer.period > 0) {
      timer.expiry = now_ms + timer.period;
      push(heap, timer);
    }
  }
}
```

## Testing Considerations
Run mixed CPU-bound and timer-heavy workloads simultaneously. Measure wake latency (actual vs. expected wake time) and jitter (variance in wake intervals) to validate drift prevention and real-time guarantees.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about live-code updates in a running kernel, including synchronization, cache coherence, and control of instruction fetch paths under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Live patching of executable memory without stopping workers
- Per-page state machines and atomic publish
- I-cache/TLB synchronization and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (pseudo-code)
enum { STABLE, STAGED, ACTIVE } state_t;
typedef struct { void *addr; void *code; state_t s; volatile int refcnt; uint64_t ver; } page_t;

int publish_patch(page_t *p, void *new_code) {
  p->s = STAGED;
  p->code = new_code;
  __sync_synchronize();
  if (atomic_compare_exchange(&p->s, STAGED, ACTIVE)) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&p->ver, 1);
    icache_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    tlb_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    return 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you guarantee rollback if a patch proves faulty?
- How to test latency under burst patch arrivals and mixed workloads?
